sking  questions and challenging  all the way through i think when  something  like iraq where people felt they'd been  misled  um it's important to have  uh a reckoning of some sort um so i  think it is a it was a healthy  thing to do i wouldn't particularly  advocate it for every conflict  it's not a cheap thing to do it takes a  lot of time  and you may end up just rediscovering  what you already knew  but in this case i think it was  important that people had a full  description a really full description  from which they could necessarily form  their own judgments about what happened  uh and why it had happened so in that  sense i think it was useful  uh a good thing to do um but you know  these these things should only be  undertaken for  special cases rather than just as a  matter of course  and so would you say it was more about  the general public's  feelings or the access to knowledge  about the war rather than  the government learning from lessons  well i think the word i mean it's a fair  point  and i think we did  make recommendations and draw some  lessons  about can you influence an american  administration  uh how do you prepare uh for  the aftermath of a conflict um  some issues to do with military planning  and logistics they're also doing the  things it would be nice to think the  government might learn from and i know  some bits of government have been trying  to learn from but i think  you know the reason for the inquiry was  that  um it was premised on the assumption  that there were  weapons of mass destruction in iraq  which were never found and  certainly didn't exist and secondly  um that it dragged on a long time  and was very painful not least for the  iraqis and  the blood of our own service people were  killed  um and so i think there was a feeling  that something had gone  wrong in terms of both what had been  promised and unintended consequences  so in that sense uh it was addressing  public concern  would you be able to go into a little  bit more detail about how you said that  some parts of government are trying to  learn from the lessons in what ways is  that happening well the ministry of  defense has produced a  sort of little little booklet about  things that you need to take into  account with many any military operation  um and i think one of the conclusions we  were trying to push  was that if you were in government  whether in the military or a civil  servant and you were bothered  um then you really should challenge and  you should feel able to challenge  what was going on you know if everybody  believes something that turns out to be  completely wrong and there was a bit of  that in this case  uh then the challenge may be limited but  in some areas people were worried  had concerns uh and didn't quite know  how to  express them and i you know be good to  think the  the if we ever were doing something like  this again  which i'm not sure we will but if we  ever did um  that people had concerns they would feel  able to  say uh um  really isn't a very good idea or have  you thought of this or why don't you try  that  um there's limits to how far that can go  in the end somebody's got to take  responsibility  uh and that may involve ignoring  reasonable concerns but at least they  should know that  those concerns are there and that if  they how do decide to ignore them  they're doing so explicitly  and looking to the future you've done  work on how you see  war will progress in the future moving  more to civil wars rather than  international wars what is your theory  of the future  i don't have a theory i mean the the the  the future of war  is as likely as not to look pretty much  like the pastor of war  um in the last few decades  civil wars have become more important  than interstates interstate wars were  always  you know few and far between very nasty  when they occurred  but civil wars are pretty constant  there are some on all the time and some  of them are very bloody indeed  and 