ve step  you could well find yourself in a  complete conflagration that would  demolish your country as well as much  else then you're going to be a little  bit more cautious  than if you think there's costs and  risks but  maybe they're worth taking so i think  what nuclear weapons have done is it  makes it harder to conceive  of major powers going to war or even  nuclear power say india and pakistan go  into war though we've got close to that  in recent times but  other forms of conflict will will carry  on and possibly become more important  simply because um the big wars  are aren't going to happen now so  you may the major powers may try to  pursue their interest through support of  factions in elsewhere rather rather than  take on their opponents directly and  you've talked slightly about the sort of  failure  or that we haven't found a way of  stopping war  do you think uh a sort of global  framework like the un  will still be relevant if we're seeing  civil wars uh  well it can be relevant um it can be  more relevant with civil wars than other  types  a lot of peacekeeping forces have been  sent in but unfortunately  you know they're not disinterested  and in some cases they've made it worse  rather than better um  [Music]  if you produce armies of not  particularly well paid  young men  they don't necessarily behave well in  the countries which they're going  um and not always very well disciplined  so  uh so they're further so firstly yes  there is a  the un can do things can organize  conferences  try to establish uh peaceful settlements  uh try to support them through  peacekeeping in some cases it's done  that quite well which wouldn't be  too negative but there are limits in  what it can do  and probably the limits are feel a bit  more  strong people are more aware of the  limits at the moment than the past was  greater optimism in the past and now  there's certain areas of the world that  you predict will be sort of  the highest conflict zones well it