ar strategists that you have  particularly inspired you and you would  recommend to others  um well  i think there's there's a lot of good  writing historical writing about war i  think  probably this this period has produced  this last 10 years has produced some of  the best military histories  about the second world war and so on  because  i think what's happened is that  there's been a greater readiness to look  at  the experience of war of individuals so  that you marry  um war as lived experience for those on  the battlefield or those in the home  front or wherever  so i i think that there's a  a lot to be said for books which now  look at  war as lived experience whether in  battle  you know on the home front as against  just  but you need this as well what were the  strategies being followed by the by the  powers what led them into war why did  they do it the way they did it  um you need that as well so i think the  the good military history  uh marries that and you know it  this country anthony b the max hastings  uh james holland i mean there and then  many others are good  with this sort of military strategy  interestingly people still go back to  classics  clouds of its on war uh  produced uh early 1830s  posthumously by his wife it's still a  remarkable  book in a powerful book about the  different  the interaction interplay of  politics military capability popular  will  battlefield conditions it's a very  powerful piece of work in recent times  the economist tom schelling  who was largely  trying to engage with nuclear issues  genuinely innovative thinker  in helping us understand the extent to  which even  people that conflict with each other  sometimes have shared interests and that  can shape the way  that they fight so that they don't fight  to the death they understand  that that's not what they want to do so  they try to find ways  of bargaining uh that don't involve  complete catastrophe and finally if you  had the chance to  set the curriculum 